<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilica of Superga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a>–<a href="/years/1731">1731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basilica of Superga is a baroque church on a hill east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a> and built in 1717–1731. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> had vowed to build it during the French siege of Turin in 1706, and it became the burial place of the house of Savoy. In 1949 an aircraft carrying the Torino football team crashed into the hill below it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga.jpg" alt="Basilica of Superga" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paris Orlando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_Superga_(Turin).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
